{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790538216,"nonce":"87UeqYTUyDHBGUCSOpr7go/apDc=","title":"wasm AOT: an import the runtime cannot supply binds 0 instead of failing","message":"A wasm AOT build accepts an import of a module the wasm runtime object does not have, and the program runs with the imported name bound to 0 (a Num). The first use then fails far from the cause:\n\n    import \"isolate\" for Isolate\n    System.print(Isolate)        // 0\n    System.print(Isolate.cpus)   // Error: Num does not implement 'cpus'\n\nNatively, the same program prints Isolate and the CPU count, and `import \"nosuchmod\" for X` fails with \"Could not load module 'nosuchmod'\". Under wasm AOT, nosuchmod builds, and X prints 0.\n\nAffected:\n- The built-in modules registered only with the host feature: fs, os, hash, crypto, zip, isolate, thread, socket, http, proc. Checked with wlift --aot --aot-target wasm32-wasip1 and wasmtime: each imports \"ok\", and its class reads 0.\n- Any module the walker cannot find.\n- The browser prelude names (Browser, Future, Dom, WebSocket, LocalStorage, SessionStorage). They are in PRELUDE_NAMES, so sema accepts them and wlift_aot_init_prelude leaves their slots unset, so they read 0. Natively they are \"undefined variable\".\n\nWhy:\n- The bootstrap ignores wlift_aot_resolve_runtime_import's return code (70 when unresolved), so the slot keeps its initial bits.\n- Module variable blobs are zero-filled, and zero bits are the Num 0, not null.\n\nFix direction: fail at build time where the build can know, and at startup otherwise, never with a silent 0.\n- A wasm AOT build rejects an import of a host-only built-in module, or of a module it cannot find, naming the import and saying the module is not in the wasm runtime.\n- Sema for a wasm target does not accept the browser prelude names outside the embedded runtime's build.\n- The bootstrap checks the runtime-import result and ends with \"Could not load module\" as the interpreter does.\n- Module variable blobs start as null rather than zero bits.\n\nEverything else checked matches the native run under wasm AOT: typed arrays, SIMD, and the modules the wasm runtime has (meta, random, time, io, regex, uuid, toml, hatch).\n- ByteArray, Int32Array, Float32Array, Float64Array: indexing, fromList/fromString, iteration, readU16LE.\n- Simd4f and Simd4i: new, splat, arithmetic, min, load/store against a Float32Array, shuffle, select.\n- Meta.eval runs, since the runtime object carries the interpreter.","files":null}]}